          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why You Should Seal Your Concrete Pavers
          </h2>
          <p>
            Concrete pavers are essentially molded concrete, which means they are full of microscopic pores that drink up water, salt, oil, and grime. From the day they are installed, those pores let UV rays bleach the surface from a rich charcoal or terracotta down to a chalky gray, while absorbed water freezes, expands, and spalls the surface in cold climates. Every rainfall and every pressure wash erodes a little more sand out of the joints — which is how weeds and ants colonize the gaps and how individual pavers begin to rock and shift underfoot.
          </p>
          <p>
            A quality <strong>concrete paver sealer</strong> solves all of these problems at once. It creates a barrier that repels water, de-icing salt, and stains, it stabilizes the polymeric sand in the joints so it stops washing out, and — with a wet-look product — it restores the deep, saturated color that sun-faded pavers lose over time. The cost is modest: roughly <strong>$80-150 in sealer</strong> for an average patio or walkway, reapplied every two to three years for film-forming products or every three to five years for penetrating ones. That is cheap insurance for a surface that can cost $15-30 per square foot to tear out and replace.
          </p>

