        <div id="how-to-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Seal Concrete Pavers: The Complete DIY Guide
          </h2>
          <p>
            Sealing concrete pavers is a satisfying weekend project, but the porous nature of concrete makes a few steps non-negotiable. Here's the process that gives professional results:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Clean Thoroughly</h3>
          <p>
            Pressure wash the pavers to remove dirt, algae, efflorescence, and grime. Treat any oil, rust, or tannin stains with a dedicated paver cleaner. Pull weeds from the joints. The surface must be spotless — sealer bonds to clean concrete, not to the film of dirt and organic growth that builds up over the seasons.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Re-Sand the Joints</h3>
          <p>
            Top up any joints that have lost sand with fresh polymeric sand, sweeping it in and compacting it just below the chamfer of the paver. Film-forming and joint-stabilizing sealers will lock this sand in place, so getting the joints full now is what stops future washout, weeds, and shifting pavers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Let It Dry Completely</h3>
          <p>
            This is the step people skip and regret. Pavers must be <strong>bone dry</strong> — ideally 24-48 hours after washing with no rain. Sealing over trapped moisture is the number one cause of the milky white haze that ruins acrylic sealer jobs, and porous concrete pavers hold water deep inside long after the surface looks dry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Add Anti-Slip Where Needed</h3>
          <p>
            If you're using a glossy wet-look sealer on a walkway, steps, or a pool surround, mix a micronized anti-slip additive into the sealer for those areas. This single step keeps a beautiful wet-look surface from becoming a slip hazard when it rains.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Apply Thin, Even Coats</h3>
          <p>
            Roll or spray the sealer in thin, even coats, working in manageable sections. Two thin coats beat one thick coat every time — flooding it on causes blushing and a tacky finish. With penetrating sealers, apply enough that the surface stays wet for a few minutes so it can soak in. Keep all traffic and water off the pavers for the full cure time on the label, typically 24-48 hours.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Wet vs Natural -->
        <div id="wet-vs-natural" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Wet Look vs. Natural Finish: Choosing the Right Appearance
          </h2>
          <p>
            The biggest aesthetic decision when sealing concrete pavers is whether you want a wet look or a natural finish. Both protect the paver, but they look and feel completely different, and the right answer depends on your taste and how the surface is used.
          </p>
          <p>
            A <strong>wet look concrete sealer</strong> deepens and saturates the paver color into a rich, just-rained-on appearance with a satin or glossy sheen. It's the dramatic choice that makes a tired patio look brand new, and the film also locks joint sand in place. The trade-off is that a glossy film can be slick when wet, so an anti-slip additive is smart on walkways and steps, and the finish needs recoating every couple of years.
          </p>
          <p>
            A <strong>natural finish</strong> — whether from a matte acrylic or a penetrating silane/siloxane sealer — keeps the pavers looking exactly as they do unsealed, with no added gloss and no change in grip. Penetrating sealers in particular leave nothing on the surface to peel, cloud, or become slippery, and they offer the best salt and freeze-thaw protection for driveways. The compromise is that you give up the color pop and, with penetrating products, the joint-sand lockdown. For many homeowners the decision is simple: choose wet look for a showcase patio, and choose a natural or penetrating finish for a driveway or a surface where barefoot grip and longevity matter most.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Maintenance -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Maintenance and Resealing
          </h2>
          <p>
            A concrete paver sealer is not a one-and-done treatment. Sun, traffic, and weather slowly wear the film or deplete the penetrating barrier, and a worn sealer offers no protection at all. The good news is that maintenance is straightforward once you know what to watch for.
          </p>
          <p>
            The simplest test is the water-bead test: splash a little water on the pavers and watch what happens. If it beads up and sits on the surface, your sealer is still working. If it soaks straight in and darkens the paver, the protection has worn off and it's time to reseal. Film-forming acrylics typically need a maintenance coat every <strong>2-3 years</strong>, while penetrating sealers can stretch to <strong>3-5 years</strong> or more. High-traffic driveways and surfaces in relentless sun sit at the shorter end of those ranges.
          </p>
          <p>
            Resealing is far easier than the first application because the prep is lighter. Clean the surface thoroughly, let it dry completely, and apply a fresh thin coat. Acrylics melt into the previous coat, so there's no stripping required as long as the old sealer is intact and you're using a compatible product. Avoid the temptation to over-seal — piling coat upon coat builds up a thick film that can crack, peel, and cloud. One thin maintenance coat at the right interval keeps concrete pavers looking sharp and fully protected for decades.
          </p>
        </div>

